Q: Stored procedure running multiple times in Angular application A: <p>estas usando mal el PreparedStatement</p>
<p>segun chatgpt:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Un <code>PreparedStatement</code> NO debe incluir valores inline</strong></p>
<pre><code>EXEC MiSP @a='1', @b='2'

</code></pre>
<p>❌ Esto <strong>no es un prepared statement real</strong></p>
</li>
<li><p>Estás mezclando:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>SQL ya “compilado” con valores</p>
</li>
<li><p>y además pasas <code>params</code> a <code>execute</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><p><strong>No declaras los parámetros con <code>ps.input()</code></strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>node-mssql requiere que declares cada parámetro <strong>antes de <code>prepare</code></strong></p>
</blockquote>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Solucion:</p>
<pre><code>storedproc(sp: string, params?: any, raw = false) {
    if (!sp) return Promise.reject('Stored Procedure is required')

    return new Promise(async (resolve, reject) =&gt; {
        try {
            const request = this.pool.request()

            if (params) {
                for (const key of Object.keys(params)) {
                    request.input(key, params[key])
                }
            }

            const result = await request.execute(sp)
            resolve(raw ? result : result.recordset)

        } catch (err) {
            reject(err)
        }
    })
}
</code></pre>

Q: More silent behaviour changes with C++20 three-way comparison A: <p>We ran into the same problem after switching to C++20. Here's a slightly different reproducer without the inheritance from <code>std::string</code>. The presence of <code>CiKey::operator const char*()</code> is enough to change the ordering of <code>mappings</code>.</p>
<p>(Change <code>_stricmp()</code> to <code>strcasecmp()</code> depending on your compiler.)</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;set&gt;

// Represents case insensitive keys
struct CiKey {
  CiKey(const char* str) : m_str(str) {}
  const char* c_str() const {
    return m_str;
  }
  operator const char* () const {
    return m_str;
  }
  bool operator&lt;(CiKey const&amp; other) const {
    return _stricmp(c_str(), other.c_str()) &lt; 0;
  }
private:
  const char* m_str;
};

using KeySet = std::set&lt;CiKey&gt;;
using Mapping = std::pair&lt;CiKey, int&gt;; // Same with std::tuple
using Mappings = std::set&lt;Mapping&gt;;

int main()
{
  KeySet keys{ &quot;one&quot;, &quot;two&quot;, &quot;ONE&quot;, &quot;three&quot; };
  Mappings mappings{
      { &quot;one&quot;, 1 }, { &quot;two&quot;, 2 }, { &quot;ONE&quot;, 1 }, { &quot;three&quot;, 3 }
  };
  std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Keys:\n&quot;;
  for (auto&amp;&amp; k : keys) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;  &quot; &lt;&lt; k.c_str() &lt;&lt; '\n';
  }
  std::cout &lt;&lt; '\n';
  std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Mappings:\n&quot;;
  for (auto&amp;&amp; m : mappings) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;  &quot; &lt;&lt; m.first.c_str() &lt;&lt; &quot;: &quot; &lt;&lt; m.second &lt;&lt; '\n';
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This also exhibits a similar issue, with the &quot;also three&quot; entry disappearing from <code>mappings</code> under C++20:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;set&gt;

// Represents case insensitive keys
struct CiKey {
  CiKey(const char* str) : m_str(str) {}
  const char* c_str() const {
    return m_str;
  }
  operator bool() const {
    return m_str != nullptr;
  }
  bool operator&lt;(CiKey const&amp; other) const {
    return strcmp(c_str(), other.c_str()) &lt; 0;
  }
private:
  const char* m_str;
};

using KeySet = std::set&lt;CiKey&gt;;
using Mapping = std::pair&lt;CiKey, int&gt;; // Same with std::tuple
using Mappings = std::set&lt;Mapping&gt;;

int main()
{
  KeySet keys{ &quot;one&quot;, &quot;two&quot;, &quot;three&quot;, &quot;also three&quot; };
  Mappings mappings{
      { &quot;one&quot;, 1 }, { &quot;two&quot;, 2 }, { &quot;three&quot;, 3 }, { &quot;also three&quot;, 3 }
  };
  std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Keys:\n&quot;;
  for (auto&amp;&amp; k : keys) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;  &quot; &lt;&lt; k.c_str() &lt;&lt; '\n';
  }
  std::cout &lt;&lt; '\n';
  std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Mappings:\n&quot;;
  for (auto&amp;&amp; m : mappings) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;  &quot; &lt;&lt; m.first.c_str() &lt;&lt; &quot;: &quot; &lt;&lt; m.second &lt;&lt; '\n';
  }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Query a list LINQ-style in PowerShell A: <p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/2870231/45375">Doug Finke's helpful answer</a> and <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/2870134/45375">Keith Hill's helpful answer</a> show you PowerShell-idiomatic analogs to the <code>.Distinct()</code> and <code>.Where()</code> LINQ methods.</p>
<hr />
<p>In <strong>PowerShell v3 or higher</strong> you now <strong><em>can</em> use LINQ</strong>.</p>
<p>The solution below demonstrates that LINQ can be used to solve your problem, but also shows that doing so is <strong>requires somewhat cumbersome syntax</strong> and is probably <strong>only worth the effort if you need advanced query features and/or performance matters</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li>See <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/48134048/45375">this answer</a> for a general overview of how to use LINQ from PowerShell.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<pre><code># Create the type whose instances will make up the list to filter.
# Make it implement IEquatable&lt;T&gt; with custom comparison logic that
# compares property values so that the .Distinct() LINQ method works correctly.
Add-Type -TypeDefinition @&quot;

  public class pack_code : System.IEquatable&lt;pack_code&gt;
  {
      public string code { get; set; }
      public string type { get; set; }

      // IEquatable&lt;T&gt; interface implementation

      // Test equality of this object with another of the same type.
      public bool Equals(pack_code other) {
        return other != null &amp;&amp; this.code == other.code &amp;&amp; this.type == other.type;
      }

      // If Equals() returns true for a pair of objects  
      // then GetHashCode() must return the same value for these objects.         
      public override int GetHashCode() {
        return code?.Length ?? 0 + type?.Length ?? 0;
        // Alternatives (tip of the hat to Santiago Squarzon):
        // * Convenient, but expensive: relies on ValueTuple implementing IEquatable&lt;T&gt; itself.
        //   return new ValueTuple&lt;string, string&gt;(code, type).GetHashCode();
        // * Convenient and better-performing, but requires PowerShell (Core) 7 (.NET 7+):
        //   return HashCode.Combine(code, type);
      }
  }

&quot;@

# Create the list to filter.
# Note:
#  * Despite not having a constructor for [pack_code], PowerShell is smart
#    enough to construct an instance from a cast from a hashtable that contains
#    entries whose names match the settable [pack_code] properties.
#  * The array of [pack_code] instances is then cast to the list type.
#  * The list contains 3 objects of type 1, but only 2 distinct ones.
$codes = [System.Collections.Generic.List[pack_code]] (
           [pack_code] @{code = '2'; type = '1'},
           [pack_code] @{code = '3'; type = '5'},
           [pack_code] @{code = '2'; type = '1'},
           [pack_code] @{code = '1'; type = '1'}
         )

# Invoke the LINQ methods as static methods of the 
# [System.Linq.Enumerable] type to
# return all distinct objects whose type property is ‘1’.
# Note that the result will be an *iterator*; if you want a
# static array, wrap the call in [Linq.Enumerable]::ToArray(...)
[Linq.Enumerable]::Where(
  [Linq.Enumerable]::Distinct($codes),
  [Func[pack_code, bool]] { $Args[0].type -eq '1' }
)
</code></pre>

Q: Reading .vtk file A: <p>If you desire to call the compiled programm from the example given from vtk-wiki simply open up a shell/dos window and type:</p>

<pre><code>yourExecutable.exe path-to-file.vtk
</code></pre>

<p>As the output stated above, you did not match the requirements for the example to run (2 parameters).</p>

<p>One parameter (the first) is the usage (to what program you call) and the second one containing the path to the vtk-file you want to read.</p>

<p>If you don't want to call it with parameters you could change the given example to this:</p>

<pre><code>int main ( int argc, char *argv[] )
{

  // simply set filename here (oh static joy)
  std::string inputFilename = "setYourPathToVtkFileHere";

  // Get all data from the file
  vtkSmartPointer&lt;vtkGenericDataObjectReader&gt; reader = 
      vtkSmartPointer&lt;vtkGenericDataObjectReader&gt;::New();
  reader-&gt;SetFileName(inputFilename.c_str());
  reader-&gt;Update();

  // All of the standard data types can be checked and obtained like this:
  if(reader-&gt;IsFilePolyData())
    {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "output is a polydata" &lt;&lt; std::endl;
    vtkPolyData* output = reader-&gt;GetPolyDataOutput();
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "output has " &lt;&lt; output-&gt;GetNumberOfPoints() &lt;&lt; " points." &lt;&lt; std::endl;
    }

  return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
</code></pre>

<p>and simply replace <em>setYourPathToVtkFileHere</em> with the (preferably absolute) your path.</p>

Q: Registering a strategy A: <p>As mentioned in the correct <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79887590/3744182">answer</a> by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/4347580/null">null</a>, you are getting that compilation error because there is no <code>RegisterType()</code> <a href="https://github.com/Catel/Catel/blob/6.1.4/src/Catel.Core/IoC/ServiceLocator.cs#L337" rel="nofollow noreferrer">method overload</a> for <code>ServiceLocator</code> or <a href="https://github.com/Catel/Catel/blob/6.1.4/src/Catel.Core/IoC/Extensions/ServiceLocatorExtensions.cs#L247" rel="nofollow noreferrer">extension method overload</a> for <code>IServiceLocator</code> with the signature shown in your question.  But even if you were to correct your code to the most plausible alternative:</p>
<pre><code>ServiceLocator.Default.RegisterType&lt;IReadOnlyDictionary&lt;string, Func&lt;ISelectPaymentViewStrategy&gt;&gt;&gt;(
    (ITypeFactory t, ServiceLocatorRegistration s) =&gt; new Dictionary&lt;string, Func&lt;ISelectPaymentViewStrategy&gt;&gt;(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
    {
        { &quot;M&quot;, () =&gt; ServiceLocator.Default.GetDependencyResolver().GetService&lt;MonthPaymentInfo&gt;() },
        { &quot;J&quot;, () =&gt; ServiceLocator.Default.GetDependencyResolver().GetService&lt;QuarterAndYearPaymentInfo&gt;() }    
    });
</code></pre>
<p>You would get another compiler error</p>
<blockquote>
<p><code>'IDependencyResolver' does not contain a definition for 'GetService' and no accessible extension method 'GetService' accepting a first argument of type 'IDependencyResolver' could be found (are you missing a using directive or an assembly reference?)</code></p>
</blockquote>
<p>So, let's take a step back.  It seems as though you want to register multiple concrete implementations (here <code>MonthPaymentInfo</code> and <code>QuarterAndYearPaymentInfo</code>) for an some interface (here <code>ISelectPaymentViewStrategy</code>) and differentiate them by <strong>ordinal-case-invariant name</strong> (here &quot;M&quot; or &quot;J&quot;).  You're trying to do this by registering a dictionary of factory methods.  However, you don't need to do this, because Catel <strong>already</strong> has functionality to do what you need.  All of its <a href="https://github.com/Catel/Catel/blob/6.1.4/src/Catel.Core/IoC/Extensions/ServiceLocatorExtensions.cs#L247" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Register</code></a> and <a href="https://github.com/Catel/Catel/blob/6.1.4/src/Catel.Core/IoC/Extensions/DependencyResolverExtensions.cs#L34C1-L37C10" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Resolve</code></a> methods have versions to pass in an additional tag, e.g.:</p>
<blockquote>
<pre><code>public static void RegisterType&lt;TServiceImplementation&gt;(this IServiceLocator serviceLocator, RegistrationType registrationType = RegistrationType.Singleton)
{
    RegisterTypeWithTag&lt;TServiceImplementation&gt;(serviceLocator, null, registrationType);
}
</code></pre>
</blockquote>
<p>and</p>
<blockquote>
<pre><code>public static T? Resolve&lt;T&gt;(this IDependencyResolver dependencyResolver, object? tag = null)
{
    return (T?)dependencyResolver.Resolve(typeof(T), tag);
}
</code></pre>
</blockquote>
<p>If you pass an appropriate strategy name into these methods, Catel will take care of injecting the correct named strategy for you.</p>
<p>Since you want to ensure your names are case invariant, you could create a couple of wrapper extension methods like the following:</p>
<pre><code>public static class CatelExtensions
{
    public static void RegisterTypeWithInvariantTag&lt;TService, TServiceImplementation&gt;(this IServiceLocator serviceLocator, string tag, RegistrationType registrationType = RegistrationType.Singleton, bool registerIfAlreadyRegistered = true)
        where TService : notnull
        where TServiceImplementation : TService
        =&gt;  serviceLocator.RegisterTypeWithTag&lt;TService, TServiceImplementation&gt;(tag.ToUpperInvariant(), registrationType, registerIfAlreadyRegistered);

    public static T? ResolveWithInvariantTag&lt;T&gt;(this IDependencyResolver dependencyResolver, string tag) =&gt;
        dependencyResolver.Resolve&lt;T&gt;(tag.ToUpperInvariant());
}
</code></pre>
<p>And then in startup, register your strategies as follows (passing whatever <code>RegistrationType</code> you need):</p>
<pre><code>ServiceLocator.Default.RegisterTypeWithInvariantTag&lt;ISelectPaymentViewStrategy, MonthPaymentInfo&gt;(&quot;M&quot;);
ServiceLocator.Default.RegisterTypeWithInvariantTag&lt;ISelectPaymentViewStrategy, QuarterAndYearPaymentInfo&gt;(&quot;J&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>(If you need to register dependencies at the instance or type level, see <a href="https://docs.catelproject.com/vnext/catel-core/ioc/introduction-to-components/dependencyresolver/#managing-the-dependency-resolvers-per-instance" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Managing the dependency resolvers per instance</a> or <a href="https://docs.catelproject.com/vnext/catel-core/ioc/introduction-to-components/dependencyresolver/#managing-the-dependency-resolvers-per-type" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Managing the dependency resolvers per type</a>.)</p>
<p>Then, when resolving your strategy (e.g. for some <code>MyViewModel myViewModel</code> type), you can retrieve your injected dependencies by following the pattern from <a href="https://docs.catelproject.com/vnext/catel-core/ioc/introduction-to-components/dependencyresolver/#retrieving-a-dependency-resolver-for-an-instance" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Retrieving a dependency resolver for an instance</a>:</p>
<pre><code>var dependencyResolverManager = DependencyResolverManager.Default;
var dependencyResolver = dependencyResolverManager.GetDependencyResolverForInstance(myViewModel);
var strategy = dependencyResolver.ResolveWithInvariantTag&lt;ISelectPaymentViewStrategy&gt;(&quot;J&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>This all assumes you know the set of strategy names (&quot;M&quot; and &quot;J&quot;) in advance.  If not, you could register them also in a separate type.  First define the following interface:</p>
<pre><code>public interface ISelectPaymentViewStrategyNames
{
    public IReadOnlyList&lt;string&gt; Names { get; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The in startup, define the following concrete implementation and register everything like so:</p>
<pre><code>public class SelectPaymentViewStrategyNames : ISelectPaymentViewStrategyNames
{
    public const string MonthPaymentInfoName = &quot;M&quot;;
    public const string QuarterAndYearPaymentInfoName = &quot;J&quot;;

    public IReadOnlyList&lt;string&gt; Names { get; } = new [] { MonthPaymentInfoName, QuarterAndYearPaymentInfoName };
}

ServiceLocator.Default.RegisterType&lt;ISelectPaymentViewStrategyNames, SelectPaymentViewStrategyNames&gt;();
ServiceLocator.Default.RegisterTypeWithInvariantTag&lt;ISelectPaymentViewStrategy, MonthPaymentInfo&gt;(SelectPaymentViewStrategyNames.MonthPaymentInfoName);
ServiceLocator.Default.RegisterTypeWithInvariantTag&lt;ISelectPaymentViewStrategy, QuarterAndYearPaymentInfo&gt;(SelectPaymentViewStrategyNames.QuarterAndYearPaymentInfoName);
</code></pre>
<p>Then you will be able to consume all of them as follows:</p>
<pre><code>var dependencyResolverManager = DependencyResolverManager.Default;
var dependencyResolver = dependencyResolverManager.GetDependencyResolverForInstance(myViewModel);

var strategies = dependencyResolver.Resolve&lt;ISelectPaymentViewStrategyNames&gt;();
if (strategies == null)
    throw new Exception(&quot;ISelectPaymentViewStrategyNames not found&quot;);
foreach (var name in strategies.Names)
{
    var strategy = dependencyResolver.ResolveWithInvariantTag&lt;ISelectPaymentViewStrategy&gt;(name);
    // Do whatever you need to do with the strategy.
}
</code></pre>
<p>Or, if you really want a dictionary, I'd suggest building it when resolving your dependencies e.g. with an extension method like so:</p>
<pre><code>public static class ModelExtensions
{
    public static IDictionary&lt;string, ISelectPaymentViewStrategy&gt; GetPaymentStrategies&lt;TViewModel&gt;(this TViewModel model) where TViewModel : notnull
    {
        var dependencyResolver = DependencyResolverManager.Default.GetDependencyResolverForInstance(model);
        var strategies = dependencyResolver.Resolve&lt;ISelectPaymentViewStrategyNames&gt;();
        if (strategies == null)
            throw new Exception(&quot;ISelectPaymentViewStrategyNames not found&quot;);
        return strategies.Names
            .Select(name =&gt; (name, strategy : dependencyResolver.ResolveWithInvariantTag&lt;ISelectPaymentViewStrategy&gt;(name)))
            .Where(t =&gt; t.strategy != null) // Or throw an exception?
            .ToDictionary(t =&gt; t.name, t =&gt; t.strategy!, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>And then you can do:</p>
<pre><code>var strategies = myViewModel.GetPaymentStrategies();
</code></pre>
